==English==

===Etymology===
{{confix|en|iso|morphism}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|en-us-isomorphism.ogg|Audio (US)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# Similarity of form
#* '''1984''' Brigitte Asbach-Schnitker, "Introduction", ''Mercury or The Secret and Swift Messenger'', {{ISBN|9027232768}}.
#*: The postulated '''isomorphism''' between words and things constitutes the characterizing feature of all philosophically based universal languages.
:# {{lb|en|biology}} the [[similarity]] in [[form]] of [[organism]]s, which may be due to [[convergent evolution]] or shared genetic background, e.g. an algae species in which the [[haploid]] and [[diploid]] life stages are indistinguishable based on morphology.
:# {{lb|en|chemistry}} the similarity in the [[crystal]] structures of similar [[chemical]] [[compound]]s
:#* '''1874''' C. Rammelsberg, [http://books.google.co.uk/books?id=jOUEAAAAQAAJ&pg=PA197#v=onepage&q&f=true "Crystallographic and chemical relations of the natural sulphides, arsenides, and sulpharsenides"], ''The Chemical News and Journal of Physical Science'', page 197.
:#*: The '''isomorphism''' of compounds does not prove the '''isomorphism''' of their respective constituents.
:# {{lb|en|sociology}} the similarity in the structure or [[processes]] of different [[organization]]s
: &nbsp;2.&nbsp;&nbsp;A one-to-one correspondence
:# {{lb|en|group algebra}} A [[bijection]] ''f'' such that both ''f'' and its [[inverse function|inverse]] ''f''<sup>&nbsp;&minus;1</sup> are [[homomorphism]]s, that is, structure-preserving mappings.
:# {{lb|en|computer science}} a [[one-to-one]] [[correspondence]] between all the [[element]]s of two [[set]]s, e.g. the [[instance]]s of two [[class]]es, or the [[record]]s in two [[dataset]]s
:# {{lb|en|category theory}} A [[morphism]] which has an inverse; the composition of the morphism and its inverse yields either one of two identity morphisms (depending on the order of composition).

